 { Thanks to his powerful connected passed pawns White has a huge advantage. However, Black has a dangerous plan in ...g7-g5! followed by ...Rg6 - Speelman. So before pushing the passed pawns forward, Alekhine rules out this option. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra5 $1 ( { In any case, the straightforward } 1. e5 { was also possible, followed by d3-d4. } ) 
Rb6 2. d4 { White's further play is rather automatic: a gradual advance of the pawns, accompanied by the king. } Rb3+ 3. Kf4 Rb4 4. d5 ( { Of course not } 4. Ke5 $4 Rb5 $1 { and Black transposes to a pawn ending where the separated passed pawns prove much stronger than their connected counterparts. } ) 
Rb6 5. Rc5 { Bringing the rook to a more active post on a7. } ( 5. e5 $2 { would be premature in view of } Rb5 $1 { , forcing a very welcome pawn trade since the pawn endgame is again unadvisable for White, e.g. } 6. Rxb5 $2 axb5 7. e6 Kf6 $1 { . } ) 
 ( { However, } 5. Ke5 $5 { in the spirit of 'king first!' could also be played before improving the rook, as observed by c0rias. At this point, } g5 { is easily refuted by } 6. Kf5 Rg6 7. Rc5 { , heading for c7. } ) 
Rb4 ( { After } 5... Rb7 6. e5 Ra7 7. Rc8 a5 8. e6 { White wins easily - Speelman. } ) 
6. Rc7+ Kf8 7. Ra7 { In this hopeless position, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

