 { Now let's see a few examples where one side succeeds in creating the fearful connected passed pawns.White has an extra pawn but Black is rather active. Alekhine finds a surprising solution. } 1. { -KEY- } b4+ $1 { A clever pawn sacrifice that diverts the black king from the defence of the e5-pawn. } ( { In contrast, } 1. Re6 $2 Rh3 2. Ke2 Kd4 { would yield Black excellent drawing chances. } ) 
Kc4 ( { After } 1... Kxb4 $6 2. Re6 { White wins with less effort. } ) 
2. Rd6 $1 { Played with the strong idea of Rd5, while simultaneously cutting off the black king from the d-file. } Rh3+ 3. Ke2 Rh4 ( { Or } 3... Rh2 4. Kf3 Rh3 5. Kg4 $1 Re3 6. f6 Rxe4 7. Kf5 Rf4 8. Kxe5 { followed by Rd4-f4. } ) 
4. Kf3 h5 5. Re6 ( 5. Rd5 $6 { has the drawback of allowing } Rf4 6. Ke3 Rxf5 { as noticed by cloots. Even if the pawn endgame arising after } 7. exf5 Kxd5 8. c4 $1 { is objectively won for White thanks to their faster passed pawns, in practical play there is absolutely no reason to enter these complications, having the much simpler 5.Re6 at hand. } ) 
Rf4+ ( { On } 5... Kd4 { White had prepared } 6. c3 $1 Kxc3 7. Rxe5 { with an easy win. } ) 
6. Ke3 h4 7. Rxe5 { The rest is now easy - the lonely h-pawn is no match for the connected passed pawns. } h3 8. Rd5 Rh4 9. Rd4+ ( { The immediate } 9. Rd1 { was also possible, as noticed by ItzRadim. } ) 
Kc3 10. Rd1 h2 11. Rh1 Rh3+ 12. Kf4 Rh4+ 13. Ke5 Kd2 14. f6 Ke3 15. Kd6 $1 Rxe4 16. Rxh2 Rd4+ 17. Ke5 
 1-0

